  One key, [38;5;153mapp.preferences[39m, holding [38;5;153m{version: 1, prefs: {...}}[39m. Reads parse the whole document; writes serialize it
  back. Migrations are one function keyed off [38;5;153mversion[39m.
  [3mBuys:[0m the cleanest migration story, and trivial export/import of "all my settings."
  [3mCosts:[0m every write rewrites every preference, so a stale tab can silently clobber another tab's change. One malformed
  blob loses every setting at once. For a login page that's survivable; as settings spread across forms, the
  whole-document write is the part that bites.

  [1mC. Thin key/value wrapper, no schema[0m
  [38;5;153mgetPreference(key, fallback)[39m / [38;5;153msetPreference(key, value)[39m, with defaults passed in at each call site.
  [3mBuys:[0m the least code by a wide margin; genuinely the right answer if this stayed one preference forever.
  [3mCosts:[0m you told me other settings and forms are coming. Call-site defaults duplicate and drift, nothing validates what
  comes back out of storage, and there's no version marker — so the first time you need to change a stored shape, you
  can't tell old data from new. That's the failure this design exists to prevent.

  [1mI recommend A.[0m It's barely more code than C and it puts the registry — the thing later forms consume — at the center
  instead of leaving it implicit. Against B: per-key writes avoid the cross-tab clobber, and I'd rather lose one bad
  preference than all of them.

  One deliberate piece of not-yet-needed machinery I'd keep in A: the per-value version marker. Code can be added later;
  [3mdata already sitting in users' browsers cannot be retroactively versioned[0m. It's a few bytes now against an
  unmigratable store later. Everything else I'd cut to YAGNI.
